 codes 0-2047 are reserved for Microsoft Corporation, and// 2048-4095 are reserved for customers.////// Macro to extract device type out of the device io control code////// Macro to extract buffering method out of the device io control code////// Define the method codes for how buffers are passed for I/O and FS controls////// Define some easier to comprehend aliases://   METHOD_DIRECT_TO_HARDWARE (writes, aka METHOD_IN_DIRECT)//   METHOD_DIRECT_FROM_HARDWARE (reads, aka METHOD_OUT_DIRECT)////// Define the access check value for any access////// The FILE_READ_ACCESS and FILE_WRITE_ACCESS constants are also defined in// ntioapi.h as FILE_READ_DATA and FILE_WRITE_DATA. The values for these// constants *MUST* always be in sync.////// FILE_SPECIAL_ACCESS is checked by the NT I/O system the same as FILE_ANY_ACCESS.// The file systems, however, may add additional access checks for I/O and FS controls// that use this value.//// end_ntddk end_wdm end_nthal end_ntifs//// IoControlCode values for storage devices////// The following device control codes are common for all class drivers.  They// should be used in place of the older IOCTL_DISK, IOCTL_CDROM and IOCTL_TAPE// common codes////// This IOCTL allows a custom request to be sent directly to a StorMQ miniport. Input and output buffer formats are established by the miniport writer.////// This IOCTL includes the same information as IOCTL_STORAGE_GET_DEVICE_NUMBER, plus the device GUID.////// This IOCTL retrieves reliability counters for a device.////// IOCTLs 0x0463 to 0x0468 reserved for dependent disk support.////// IOCTLs 0x0470 to 0x047f reserved for device and stack telemetry interfaces////// IOCTLs 0x0503 to 0x0580 reserved for Enhanced Storage devices.////// This IOCTL offloads the erasure process to the storage device. There is no guarantee as to the successful// deletion or recoverability of the data on the storage device after command completion. This IOCTL is limited// to data disks in regular Windows. In WinPE, this IOCTL is supported for both boot and data disks.//// This IOCTL has an optional input and returns no output other than status. Callers should first call// FSCTL_LOCK_VOLUME before calling this ioctl to flush out cached data in upper layers. No waiting of outstanding// request completion is done before issuing the command to the device.////// IOCTLs for bandwidth contracts on storage devices// (Move this to ntddsfio if we decide to use a new base)////// IOCTL to check for priority support////// IOCTL for data integrity check support////// These ioctl codes are obsolete.  They are defined here to avoid resuing them// and to allow class drivers to respond to them more easily.////// IOCTLs 0x0643 to 0x0655 reserved for VHD disk support.////// IOCTLs for firmware upgrade on storage devices////// IOCTL to support Idle Power Management, including Device Wake////// IOCTLs to allow class drivers to acquire and release active references on// a unit.  These should only be used if the class driver previously sent a// successful IOCTL_STORAGE_ENABLE_IDLE_POWER request to the port driver.////// This IOCTL indicates that the physical device has triggered some sort of event.////// IOCTL to specify a power cap for a storage device.////// IOCTL to send commands to the RPMB for a storage device.////// IOCTL to manage attributes for storage devices////// IOCTL_STORAGE_DIAGNOSTIC IOCTL to query diagnostic data from the storage driver stack////// IOCTLs for storage device depopulation support.////// IOCTL_STORAGE_GET_PHYSICAL_ELEMENT_STATUS IOCTL to query physical element status from device.////// IOCTL_STORAGE_REMOVE_ELEMENT_AND_TRUNCATE IOCTL to remove and truncate element from device.////// IOCTL_STORAGE_GET_DEVICE_INTERNAL_LOG IOCTL to get device internal status data.////// Note: Function code values of less than 0x800 are reserved for Microsoft. Values of 0x800 and higher can be used by vendors.//       So do not use function code of 0x800 and higher to define new IOCTLs in this file.////// IOCTL_STORAGE_GET_HOTPLUG_INFO//// version// ie. zip, jaz, cdrom, mo, etc. vs hdd// ie. does the device succeed a lock even though its not lockable media?// ie. 1394, USB, etc.// This field should not be relied upon because it is no longer used//// IOCTL_STORAGE_GET_SYSTEM_FEATURE_SUPPORT//// This IOCTL can be sent to any disk or adapter device but the query itself returns system-wide// feature support as offered by the currently-installed version of the storage stack.////// Size of this structure////// Version of this structure////// If set to '1', indicates that support for StorMQ miniports is present////// Reserved for future use. Must be set to zero.////// IOCTL_STORAGE_GET_DEVICE_NUMBER//// input - none//// output - STORAGE_DEVICE_NUMBER structure//          The values in the STORAGE_DEVICE_NUMBER structure are guaranteed//          to remain unchanged until the system is rebooted.  They are not//          guaranteed to be persistant across boots.////// The FILE_DEVICE_XXX type for this device.////// The number of this device////// If the device is partitionable, the partition number of the device.// Otherwise -1////// Size of this structure serves// as the version////// Size of this structure////// IOCTL_STORAGE_GET_DEVICE_NUMBER_EX//// input - none//// output - STORAGE_DEVICE_NUMBER_EX structure////// Possible flags that can be set in Flags field of// STORAGE_DEVICE_NUMBER_EX structure defined below////// This flag indicates that deviceguid is randomly created because a deviceguid conflict was observed////// This flag indicates that deviceguid is randomly created because the HW ID was not available////// This flag indicates that deviceguid is created from the scsi page83 data.// If this flag is not set this implies it's created from serial number or is randomly generated.////// Sizeof(STORAGE_DEVICE_NUMBER_EX).////// Total size of the structure, including any additional data. Currently// this will always be the same as sizeof(STORAGE_DEVICE_NUMBER_EX).////// Flags - this shall be a combination of STORAGE_DEVICE_FLAGS_XXX flags// that gives more information about the members of this structure.////// The FILE_DEVICE_XXX type for this device. This IOCTL is only// supported for disk devices.////// The number of this device.////// A globally-unique identification number for this device.// A GUID of {0} indicates that a GUID could not be generated. The GUID// is based on hardware information that doesn't change with firmware updates// (for instance, serial number can be used to form the GUID, but not the firmware// revision). The device GUID remains the same across reboots.//// In general, if a device exposes a globally unique identifier, the storage driver// will use that identifier to form the GUID. Otherwise, the storage driver will combine// the device's vendor ID, product ID and serial number to create the GUID.//// If a storage driver detects two devices with the same hardware information (which is// an indication of a problem with the device), the driver will generate a random GUID for// one of the two devices. When handling IOCTL_STORAGE_GET_DEVICE_NUMBER_EX for the device// with the random GUID, the driver will add STORAGE_DEVICE_FLAGS_RANDOM_DEVICEGUID_REASON_CONFLICT// to the Flags member of this structure.//// If a storage device does not provide any identifying information, the driver will generate a random// GUID and add STORAGE_DEVICE_FLAGS_RANDOM_DEVICEGUID_REASON_NOHWID to the Flags member of this structure.//// A random GUID is not persisted and will not be the same after a reboot.////// If the device is partitionable, the partition number of the device.// Otherwise -1.////// Define the structures for scsi resets////// Break reservation is sent to the Adapter/FDO with the given lun information.////// IOCTL_STORAGE_MEDIA_REMOVAL disables the mechanism// on a storage device that ejects media. This function// may or may not be supported on storage devices that// support removable media.//// TRUE means prevent media from being removed.// FALSE means allow media removal.//////  This is the format of TARGET_DEVICE_CUSTOM_NOTIFICATION.CustomDataBuffer//  passed to applications by the classpnp autorun code (via IoReportTargetDeviceChangeAsynchronous).//// see MEDIA_CHANGE_DETECTION_STATE enum in classpnp.h in DDK// begin_ntminitape//// IOCTL_STORAGE_GET_MEDIA_TYPES_EX will return an array of DEVICE_MEDIA_INFO// structures, one per supported type, embedded in the GET_MEDIA_TYPES struct.////// Following are defined in ntdddisk.h in the MEDIA_TYPE enum//// Unknown,                // Format is unknown// F5_1Pt2_512,            // 5.25", 1.2MB,  512 bytes/sector// F3_1Pt44_512,           // 3.5",  1.44MB, 512 bytes/sector// F3_2Pt88_512,           // 3.5",  2.88MB, 512 bytes/sector// F3_20Pt8_512,           // 3.5",  20.8MB, 512 bytes/sector// F3_720_512,             // 3.5",  720KB,  512 bytes/sector// F5_360_512,             // 5.25", 360KB,  512 bytes/sector// F5_320_512,             // 5.25", 320KB,  512 bytes/sector// F5_320_1024,            // 5.25", 320KB,  1024 bytes/sector// F5_180_512,             // 5.25", 180KB,  512 bytes/sector// F5_160_512,             // 5.25", 160KB,  512 bytes/sector// RemovableMedia,         // Removable media other than floppy// FixedMedia,             // Fixed hard disk media// F3_120M_512,            // 3.5", 120M Floppy// F3_640_512,             // 3.5" ,  640KB,  512 bytes/sector// F5_640_512,             // 5.25",  640KB,  512 bytes/sector// F5_720_512,             // 5.25",  720KB,  512 bytes/sector// F3_1Pt2_512,            // 3.5" ,  1.2Mb,  512 bytes/sector// F3_1Pt23_1024,          // 3.5" ,  1.23Mb, 1024 bytes/sector// F5_1Pt23_1024,          // 5.25",  1.23MB, 1024 bytes/sector// F3_128Mb_512,           // 3.5" MO 128Mb   512 bytes/sector// F3_230Mb_512,           // 3.5" MO 230Mb   512 bytes/sector// F8_256_128,             // 8",     256KB,  128 bytes/sector// F3_200Mb_512,           // 3.5",   200M Floppy (HiFD)//// Tape - DAT DDS1,2,... (all vendors)// Tape - miniQIC Tape// Tape - Travan TR-1,2,3,...// Tape - QIC// Tape - 8mm Exabyte Metal Particle// Tape - 8mm Exabyte Advanced Metal Evap// Tape - 8mm Sony AIT// Tape - DLT Compact IIIxt, IV// Tape - Philips NCTP// Tape - IBM 3480// Tape - IBM 3490E// Tape - IBM Magstar 3590// Tape - IBM Magstar MP// Tape - STK Data D3// Tape - Sony DTF// Tape - 6mm Digital Video// Tape - Exabyte DMI and compatibles// Tape - Sony D2S and D2L// Cleaner - All Drive types that support Drive Cleaners// Opt_Disk - CD// Opt_Disk - CD-Recordable (Write Once)// Opt_Disk - CD-Rewriteable// Opt_Disk - DVD-ROM// Opt_Disk - DVD-Recordable (Write Once)// Opt_Disk - DVD-Rewriteable// Opt_Disk - 3.5" Rewriteable MO Disk// Opt_Disk - MO 5.25" Write Once// Opt_Disk - MO 5.25" Rewriteable (not LIMDOW)// Opt_Disk - MO 5.25" Rewriteable (LIMDOW)// Opt_Disk - Phase Change 5.25" Write Once Optical// Opt_Disk - Phase Change 5.25" Rewriteable// Opt_Disk - PhaseChange Dual Rewriteable// Opt_Disk - Ablative 5.25" Write Once Optical// Opt_Disk - Pinnacle Apex 4.6GB Rewriteable Optical// Opt_Disk - Sony 12" Write Once// Opt_Disk - Philips/LMS 12" Write Once// Opt_Disk - Hitachi 12" Write Once// Opt_Disk - Cygnet/ATG 12" Write Once// Opt_Disk - Kodak 14" Write Once// Opt_Disk - Near Field Recording (Terastor)// Opt_Disk - Nikon 12" Rewriteable// Mag_Disk - Iomega Zip// Mag_Disk - Iomega Jaz// Mag_Disk - Syquest EZ135// Mag_Disk - Syquest EzFlyer// Mag_Disk - Syquest SyJet// Mag_Disk - 2.5" Floppy// Tape - 8mm Hitachi// Ampex DST Small Tapes// Ampex DST Medium Tapes// Ampex DST Large Tapes// Ecrix 8mm Tape// Ecrix 8mm Tape// STK 9840// IBM, HP, Seagate LTO Ultrium// IBM, HP, Seagate LTO Accelis// Opt_Disk - DVD-RAM// AIT2 or higher// OnStream ADR Mediatypes// STK 9940// SAIT Tapes// VXA (Ecrix 8mm) Tape//// Define the different storage bus types// Bus types below 128 (0x80) are reserved for Microsoft use////// Macro to identify which bus types// support shared storage//// Bitmask of MEDIA_XXX values.// Bitmask of MEDIA_XXX values.// Bitmask of MEDIA_XXX values.//// Bus specific information describing the medium supported.//// FILE_DEVICE_XXX values//// IOCTL_STORAGE_PREDICT_FAILURE//// input - none//// output - STORAGE_PREDICT_FAILURE structure//          PredictFailure returns zero if no failure predicted and non zero//                         if a failure is predicted.////          VendorSpecific returns 512 bytes of vendor specific information//                         if a failure is predicted////// IOCTL_STORAGE_FAILURE_PREDICTION_CONFIG//// Input - STORAGE_FAILURE_PREDICTION_CONFIG structure.//         If the sender wants to enable or disable failure prediction then//         the sender should set the "Set" field to TRUE.// Output - STORAGE_FAILURE_PREDICTION_CONFIG structure.//          If successful, the "Enabled" field will indicate if failure//          prediction is currently enabled or not.//// Set to 1 for Blue.// TRUE if the sender wants to enable/disable failure prediction.// end_ntminitape//// Property Query Structures////// IOCTL_STORAGE_QUERY_PROPERTY//// Input Buffer://      a STORAGE_PROPERTY_QUERY structure which describes what type of query//      is being done, what property is being queried for, and any additional//      parameters which a particular property query requires.////  Output Buffer://      Contains a buffer to place the results of the query into.  Since all//      property descriptors can be cast into a STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER,//      the IOCTL can be called once with a small buffer then again using//      a buffer as large as the header reports is necessary.////// Types of queries//// Retrieves the descriptor// Used to test whether the descriptor is supported// Used to retrieve a mask of writeable fields in the descriptor// use to validate the value//// IOCTL_STORAGE_SET_PROPERTY//// Input Buffer://      a STORAGE_PROPERTY_SET structure which describes what type of property set//      is being done, what property is being set, and any additional//      parameters which a particular property set requires.////  Output Buffer://      Contains a buffer to place the results of the query into.  Since all//      property descriptors can be cast into a STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER,//      the IOCTL can be called once with a small buffer then again using//      a buffer as large as the header reports is necessary.////// Types of set operation//// Sets the descriptor// Used to test whether the descriptor is supported// use to validate the value//// define some initial property id's//// See storduid.h for details// Deprecated for GE or greater OS. Use StorageHwCryptoProperty.//// Query structure - additional parameters for specific queries can follow// the header////// ID of the property being retrieved////// Flags indicating the type of query being performed////// Space for additional parameters if necessary////// Set structure - additional parameters for specific set property that can follow// the header////// ID of the property being retrieved////// Flags indicating the type of set property being performed////// Space for additional parameters if necessary////// Standard property descriptor header.  All property pages should use this// as their first element or should contain these two elements////// Device property descriptor - this is really just a rehash of the inquiry// data retrieved from a scsi device//// This may only be retrieved from a target device.  Sending this to the bus// will result in an error////// Sizeof(STORAGE_DEVICE_DESCRIPTOR)////// Total size of the descriptor, including the space for additional// data and id strings////// The SCSI-2 device type////// The SCSI-2 device type modifier (if any) - this may be zero////// Flag indicating whether the device's media (if any) is removable.  This// field should be ignored for media-less devices////// Flag indicating whether the device can support mulitple outstanding// commands.  The actual synchronization in this case is the responsibility// of the port driver.////// Byte offset to the zero-terminated ascii string containing the device's// vendor id string.  For devices with no such ID this will be zero////// Byte offset to the zero-terminated ascii string containing the device's// product id string.  For devices with no such ID this will be zero////// Byte offset to the zero-terminated ascii string containing the device's// product revision string.  For devices with no such string this will be// zero////// Byte offset to the zero-terminated ascii string containing the device's// serial number.  For devices with no serial number this will be zero////// Contains the bus type (as defined above) of the device.  It should be// used to interpret the raw device properties at the end of this structure// (if any)////// The number of bytes of bus-specific data which have been appended to// this descriptor////// Place holder for the first byte of the bus specific property data////// Adapter properties//// This descriptor can be retrieved from a target device object of from the// device object for the bus.  Retrieving from the target device object will// forward the request to the underlying bus////// Sizeof(STORAGE_ACCESS_ALIGNMENT_DESCRIPTOR)////// Total size of the descriptor, including the space for additional// data and id strings////// The number of bytes in a cache line of the device////// The address offset neccessary for proper cache access alignment in bytes////// The number of bytes in a physical sector of the device////// The number of bytes in an addressable logical sector (LBA)of the device////// The address offset neccessary for proper sector access alignment in bytes////// Sizeof(STORAGE_MEDIUM_PRODUCT_TYPE_DESCRIPTOR)////// Total size of the descriptor, including the space for additional data////// Product type of the supporting storage medium////// Storage identification descriptor.// The definitions here are based on the SCSI/SBP vital product data// device identifier page.//// Mislabeled above but need to keep it for backwards compatibility// NAA formats (Used with StorageIdTypeNAA)//// Add new fields here since existing code depends on// the above layout not changing.////// The identifier is a variable length array of bytes.////// The number of identifiers reported by the device.////// The following field is actually a variable length array of identification// descriptors.  Unfortunately there's no C notation for an array of// variable length structures so we're forced to just pretend.//// output buffer for   StorageDeviceSeekPenaltyProperty & PropertyStandardQuery// keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// output buffer for   StorageDeviceWriteAggregationProperty & PropertyStandardQuery// keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// output buffer for   StorageDeviceTrimProperty & PropertyStandardQuery// keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER//// Output buffer for StorageDeviceLBProvisioningProperty & PropertyStandardQuery//// keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// Supports DeviceDsmAction_GetFreeSpace// Supports DeviceDsmAction_Map// Granularity in bytes.// Granularity alignment in bytes.// Max LBAs that can be unmapped in a single UNMAP command, in logical blocks.// Max number of descriptors allowed in a single UNMAP command.//// IOCTL_STORAGE_GET_LB_PROVISIONING_MAP_RESOURCES//// Input Buffer://      None//// Output Buffer://      Structure of type STORAGE_LB_PROVISIONING_MAP_RESOURCES//// See LOG_PAGE_LBP_RESOURCE_SCOPE_* definitions in scsi.h for scope values.// Available LBA mapping resources, in bytes.// Used LBA mapping resources, in bytes.// output buffer for   StorageDevicePowerProperty & PropertyStandardQuery// keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// The device supports "device attention".// The device supports asynchronous notifications, delivered via IOCTL_STORAGE_EVENT_NOTIFICATION.// The device has been registered for runtime idle power management.// The device will be powered off when put into D3.// The platform supports D3Cold for this device.// Device require no verification during idle power transitions.// The idle timeout value in milliseconds. Only valid if IdlePowerManagementEnabled == TRUE.//// Output buffer for StorageDeviceCopyOffloadProperty & PropertyStandardQuery//// keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER//// Output buffer for StorageDeviceResiliencyProperty & PropertyStandardQuery////// Size of this structure serves// as the version////// Size of  this structure  plus// all the variable sized fields////// Friendly name associated with// this descriptor////// Number of  logical  copies of// data that are available////// Number of  complete copies of// data that are stored////// Number of disks that can fail// without leading to  data loss////// Number  of columns associated// with this descriptor////// Stripe  width associated with// this descriptor, in bytes////// Output buffer for StorageAdapterRpmbProperty & PropertyStandardQuery////// Keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// Shall be set to STORAGE_RPMB_DESCRIPTOR_VERSION_1////// Keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// Shall be set to sizeof(STORAGE_RPMB_DESCRIPTOR)////// The size of the RPMB, in bytes.//// 0 if not supported, RPMB size in bytes otherwise////// The maximum amount of data supported in one transaction// in bytes.//// 0 if not supported, minimum 512 bytes////// To support different RPMB frame formats, specify which// frame format the payload will be in so the port driver// can take the appropriate action//// begin_storport begin_privstorport//// Output buffer for StorageAdapterCryptoProperty & PropertyStandardQuery////// Legacy compatibility algorithm names.// Use the names above.////// Legacy compatibility key size names.// Use the names above.//// end_storport end_privstorport//// Note: Starting in Win11 24H2 and WS2025 or GE, this struct is deprecated. // Use STORAGE_HW_CRYPTO_CAPABILITY.////// To enable versioning of this structure. This shall be set// to STORAGE_CRYPTO_CAPABILITY_VERSION_1////// Size of this structure. This shall be set to// sizeof(STORAGE_CRYPTO_CAPABILITY)////// The index for this crypto capability////// Supported algorithm for this crypto capability////// The supported key size for this algorithm////// Bitmask for the supported sizes of encryptable data blocks. When bit// j is set (j=0...7), a data unit size of 512*2^j bytes is supported.// Bit 0 represents 512 bytes, 1 represents 1 KB, bit 7 represents 64 KB//// begin_storport begin_privstorport// end_storport end_privstorport//// To enable versioning of this structure. This shall be set// to STORAGE_CRYPTO_CAPABILITY_VERSION_2////// Size of this structure. This shall be set to// sizeof(STORAGE_CRYPTO_CAPABILITY_V2)////// The index for this crypto capability////// Supported algorithm for this crypto capability////// The supported key size for this algorithm////// Bitmask for the supported sizes of encryptable data blocks. When bit// j is set (j=0...7), a data unit size of 512*2^j bytes is supported.// Bit 0 represents 512 bytes, 1 represents 1 KB, bit 7 represents 64 KB////// Maximum supported initialization vector bit size. This can be 0 if// this concept does not apply to the algorithm.////// Bitmask of compliant security standards at the algorithm level.////// Note: Starting in Win11 24H2 and WS2025 or GE, this structure is deprecated.// Use STORAGE_HW_CRYPTO_DESCRIPTOR.////// Keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// Shall be set to STORAGE_CRYPTO_DESCRIPTOR_VERSION_1////// Keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// Shall be set to sizeof(STORAGE_CRYPTO_DESCRIPTOR)////// The number of keys the crypto engine in the adapter supports////// The number of crypto capability entries. This outlines the// crypto configurations the adapter supports////// Array of Crypto Capabilities////// Note: Starting in Win11 24H2 and WS2025 or GE, this structure is deprecated. // Use STORAGE_HW_CRYPTO_DESCRIPTOR.////// Keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// Shall be set to STORAGE_CRYPTO_DESCRIPTOR_VERSION_2////// Keep compatible with STORAGE_DESCRIPTOR_HEADER// Shall be set to sizeof(STORAGE_CRYPTO_DESCRIPTOR_V2)////// The number of keys the crypto engine in the adapter supports////// The number of crypto capability entries. This outlines the// crypto configurations the adapter supports////// Which type of inline crypto engine this is////// Bitmask of compliant security standards.////// Bitmask of supported key types.////// Array of Crypto Capabilities.// NOTE: You cannot index into this array.//       Instead compute the next offset as//       curCryptoCapability =//          (STORAGE_CRYPTO_CAPABILITY_V2*)((PBYTE )curCryptoCapability + curCryptoCapability->Size)////// Output buffer for StorageHwCryptoProperty////// To enable versioning of this structure. This shall be set// to STORAGE_HW_CRYPTO_CAPABILITY_VERSION_1////// Size of this structure. This shall be set to// sizeof(STORAGE_HW_CRYPTO_CAPABILITY)////// The index for this crypto capability////// Supported algorithm for this crypto capability////// The supported key size for this algorithm////// Bitmask for the supported sizes of encryptable data blocks. When bit// j is set (j=0...7), a data unit size of 512*2^j bytes is supported.// Bit 0 represents 512 bytes, 1 represents 1 KB, bit 7 represents 64 KB////// Maximum supported initialization vector bit size. This can be 0 if// this concept does not apply to the algorithm.////// Bitmask of compliant security standards at the algorithm level.////// Header.Version is set to STORAGE_HW_CRYPTO_DESCRIPTOR_VERSION_1// to enable future version updates.//// Header.Size is set