--*///// Feature register defines for SMART "sub commands"////// IOCTLs to query and modify attributes// associated with the given disk. These// are persisted within the registry.////// IOCTL_DISK_GET_DISK_ATTRIBUTES//// Input Buffer://     None//// Output Buffer://     Structure of type GET_DISK_ATTRIBUTES////// Specifies the size of the// structure for versioning.////// Reserved. Must ignore.////// Specifies the attributes// associated with the disk.////// IOCTL_DISK_SET_DISK_ATTRIBUTES//// Input Buffer://     Structure of type SET_DISK_ATTRIBUTES//// Output Buffer://     None////// Specifies the size of the// structure for versioning.////// Indicates whether to remember// these settings across reboots// or not.////// Reserved. Must set to zero.////// Specifies the new attributes.////// Specifies the attributes// that are being modified.////// Reserved. Must set to zero.////// Common structures describing elements.//// As defined by SCSI// As defined by SCSI// As defined by SCSI// As defined by SCSI// As defined by SCSI// Front panel, used to access internal of cabinet.// Keypad/input on front panel.// Placeholder only. Not a valid type.//// Definitions for  IOCTL_CHANGER_GET_PARAMETERS////// Definitions for Features0 of GET_CHANGER_PARAMETERS//// in the IE Port.// initiated by an application.// move / exchange command can be issued to the drive.// before a move / exchange command can be issued with the drive as src/dst.// and unique.//// Definitions for Features1 of GET_CHANGER_PARAMETERS////// Definitions for MoveFrom, ExchangeFrom, and PositionCapabilities////// Definitions for LockUnlockCapabilities////// Size of the structure. Can be used for versioning.////// Number of N element(s) as defined by the Element Address Page (or equivalent...).//// for data cartridges only// for cleaner cartridges//// Number of doors/front panels (allows user entry into the cabinet).////// The device-specific address (from user manual of the device) of the first N element. Used// by the UI to relate the various elements to the user.////// Indicates the capacity of each magazine, if they exist.////// Specifies the approximate number of seconds for when a cleaning should be completed.// Only applicable if drive cleaning is supported. See Features0.////// See features bits, above.////// Bitmask defining Move from N element to element. Defined by Device Capabilities Page (or equivalent).// AND-masking with the TO_XXX values will indicate legal destinations.////// Bitmask defining Exchange from N element to element. Defined by Device Capabilities Page (or equivalent).// AND-masking with the TO_XXX values will indicate legal destinations.////// Bitmask defining which elements are capable of lock/unlock. Valid only if// CHANGER_LOCK_UNLOCK is set in Features0.////// Bitmask defining which elements valid for positioning operations. Valid only if// CHANGER_POSITION_TO_ELEMENT is set in Features0.////// For future expansion.////// Definitions for IOCTL_CHANGER_GET_PRODUCT_DATA////// Device manufacturer's name - based on inquiry data////// Product identification as defined by the vendor - based on Inquiry data////// Product revision as defined by the vendor.////// Vendor unique value used to globally identify this device. Can// be from Vital Product Data, for example.////// Indicates device type of data transports, as defined by SCSI-2.////// Definitions for IOCTL_CHANGER_SET_ACCESS////// Element can be ChangerIEPort, ChangerDoor, ChangerKeypad////// See above for possible operations.////// Definitions for IOCTL_CHANGER_GET_ELEMENT_STATUS////// Input buffer.////// List describing the elements and range on which to return information.////// Indicates whether volume tag information is to be returned.////// Output buffer.////// Element to which this structure refers.////// Address of the element from which the media was originally moved.// Valid if ELEMENT_STATUS_SVALID bit of Flags DWORD is set.// Needs to be converted to a zero-based offset from the device-unique value.////// See below.////// See below for possible values.////// Scsi Target Id of this element.// Valid only if ELEMENT_STATUS_ID_VALID is set in Flags.////// LogicalUnitNumber of this element.// Valid only if ELEMENT_STATUS_LUN_VALID is set in Flags.////// Primary volume identification for the media.// Valid only if ELEMENT_STATUS_PVOLTAG bit is set in Flags.////// Alternate volume identification for the media.// Valid for two-sided media only, and pertains to the id. of the inverted side.// Valid only if ELEMENT_STATUS_AVOLTAG bit is set in Flags.////// Output buffer. This is same as CHANGER_ELEMENT_STATUS with// the addition of product info fields. New applications should// use this struct instead of the older CHANGER_ELEMENT_STATUS////// Element to which this structure refers.////// Address of the element from which the media was originally moved.// Valid if ELEMENT_STATUS_SVALID bit of Flags DWORD is set.// Needs to be converted to a zero-based offset from the device-unique value.////// See below.////// See below for possible values.////// Scsi Target Id of this element.// Valid only if ELEMENT_STATUS_ID_VALID is set in Flags.////// LogicalUnitNumber of this element.// Valid only if ELEMENT_STATUS_LUN_VALID is set in Flags.////// Primary volume identification for the media.// Valid only if ELEMENT_STATUS_PVOLTAG bit is set in Flags.////// Alternate volume identification for the media.// Valid for two-sided media only, and pertains to the id. of the inverted side.// Valid only if ELEMENT_STATUS_AVOLTAG bit is set in Flags.////// Vendor ID////// Product ID////// Serial number////// Possible flag values////// ExceptionCode values.////// Definitions for IOCTL_CHANGER_INITIALIZE_ELEMENT_STATUS////// List describing the elements and range on which to initialize.////// Indicates whether a bar code scan should be used. Only applicable if// CHANGER_BAR_CODE_SCANNER_INSTALLED is set in Features0 of CHANGER_GET_PARAMETERS.////// Definitions for IOCTL_CHANGER_SET_POSITION////// Indicates which transport to move.////// Indicates the final destination of the transport.////// Indicates whether the media currently carried by Transport, should be flipped.////// Definitions for IOCTL_CHANGER_EXCHANGE_MEDIUM////// Indicates which transport to use for the exchange operation.////// Indicates the source for the media that is to be moved.////// Indicates the final destination of the media originally at Source.////// Indicates the destination of the media moved from Destination1.////// Indicates whether the medium should be flipped.////// Definitions for IOCTL_CHANGER_MOVE_MEDIUM////// Indicates which transport to use for the move operation.////// Indicates the source for the media that is to be moved.////// Indicates the destination of the media originally at Source.////// Indicates whether the media should be flipped.////// Definitions for IOCTL_QUERY_VOLUME_TAGS////// Input buffer.////// Describes the starting element for which to return information.////// Indicates the specific action to perform. See below.////// Template used by the device to search for volume ids.////// Output buffer.////// Number of elements matching criteria set forth by ActionCode.////// Array of CHANGER_ELEMENT_STATUS structures, one for each element that corresponded// with the information passed in with the CHANGER_SEND_VOLUME_TAG_INFORMATION structure.////// Possible ActionCode values. See Features0 of CHANGER_GET_PARAMETERS for compatibility with// the current device.////// Changer diagnostic test related definitions////// The following values follow the escape designator in the// data stream if the LSRMST_INSERT mode has been turned on.////// Following this value is the contents of the line status// register, and then the character in the RX hardware when// the line status register was encountered.////// Following this value is the contents of the line status// register.  No error character follows////// Following this value is the contents of the modem status// register.////// Bit values for FIFO Control Register////// Bit values for Modem Control Register////// The following is a list of the native file system fsctls followed by// additional network file system fsctls.  Some values have been// decommissioned.//// decommissioned fsctl value                                              9// decommissioned fsctl value                                             13// decommissioned fsctl value                                             17// decommissioned fsctl value                                             18// decommissioned fsctl value                                             31// decommissioned fsctl value                                             33// decommissioned fsctl value                                             34// decommissioned fsctl value                                             36// decommissioned fsctl value                                             37// decommissioned fsctl value                                             66// decommissioned fsctl value                                             67// decommissioned fsctl value                                             68// decommissioned fsctl value                                             70// decommissioned fsctl value                                                 80// decommissioned fsctl value                                                 83// decommissioned fsctl value                                                 93// decommissioned fsctl value                                                 96// decommissioned fsctl value                                                 97// decommissioned fsctl value                                                 98// decommissioned fsctl value                                                 105////  Values 111 - 119 are reserved for FSRM.//////  Values 131 - 139 are reserved for FSRM.////// AVIO IOCTLS.//// end_ntddk// begin_ntifs//// The following long list of structs are associated with the preceding// file system fsctls.//////==================== FSCTL_IS_PATHNAME_VALID ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_IS_PATHNAME_VALID//////==================== FSCTL_QUERY_BPB_INFO ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_QUERY_BPB_INFO//////==================== FSCTL_GET_NTFS_VOLUME_DATA ======================//// Structures for FSCTL_GET_NTFS_VOLUME_DATA.// The user must pass the basic buffer below.  Ntfs// will return as many fields as available in the extended// buffer which follows immediately after the VOLUME_DATA_BUFFER.//////==================== FSCTL_GET_REFS_VOLUME_DATA ======================//// Structures for FSCTL_GET_REFS_VOLUME_DATA.//// between 0 and 10000// between 0 and 10000// in clusters per second////==================== FSCTL_GET_VOLUME_BITMAP ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_GET_VOLUME_BITMAP//////==================== FSCTL_GET_RETRIEVAL_POINTERS ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_GET_RETRIEVAL_POINTERS//////==================== FSCTL_GET_RETRIEVAL_POINTERS_AND_REFCOUNT ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_GET_RETRIEVAL_POINTERS_AND_REFCOUNT////// Input structure is STARTING_VCN_INPUT_BUFFER//////==================== FSCTL_GET_RETRIEVAL_POINTER_COUNT ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_GET_RETRIEVAL_POINTER_COUNT////// Input structure is STARTING_VCN_INPUT_BUFFER//////==================== FSCTL_GET_NTFS_FILE_RECORD ======================//// Structures for FSCTL_GET_NTFS_FILE_RECORD//////==================== FSCTL_MOVE_FILE ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_MOVE_FILE//////  32/64 Bit thunking support structure//////==================== FSCTL_FIND_FILES_BY_SID ======================//// Structures for FSCTL_FIND_FILES_BY_SID//////  The following structures apply to Usn operations.//////==================== FSCTL_ENUM_USN_DATA ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_ENUM_USN_DATA//////==================== FSCTL_CREATE_USN_JOURNAL ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_CREATE_USN_JOURNAL//////==================== FSCTL_READ_FILE_USN_DATA ====================//// Structure for FSCTL_READ_FILE_USN_DATA//////  Windows 7 and earlier releases did not use an input buffer//  for this FSCTL.  It is valid to omit this, and doing so//  will default to MinMajorVersion == 2, MaxMajorVersion == 2.//////==================== FSCTL_READ_USN_JOURNAL ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_READ_USN_JOURNAL//////==================== FSCTL_USN_TRACK_MODIFIED_RANGES ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_USN_TRACK_MODIFIED_RANGES//////  The initial Major.Minor version of the Usn record will be 2.0.//  In general, the MinorVersion may be changed if fields are added//  to this structure in such a way that the previous version of the//  software can still correctly the fields it knows about.  The//  MajorVersion should only be changed if the previous version of//  any software using this structure would incorrectly handle new//  records due to structure changes.////  The first update to this will force the structure to version 2.0.//  This will add the extended information about the source as//  well as indicate the file name offset within the structure.////  The following structure is returned with these fsctls.////      FSCTL_READ_USN_JOURNAL//      FSCTL_READ_FILE_USN_DATA//      FSCTL_ENUM_USN_DATA//////==================== FSCTL_QUERY_USN_JOURNAL ======================////  Structure for FSCTL_QUERY_USN_JOURNAL//////==================== FSCTL_DELETE_USN_JOURNAL ======================////  Structure for FSCTL_DELETE_USN_JOURNAL//////==================== FSCTL_MARK_HANDLE ======================////  Structure for FSCTL_MARK_HANDLE////Flags////  32/64 Bit thunking support structure////Flags////  Flags for the additional source information above.//  To set any of these values required a volume DASD handle to be specified in//  VolumeHandle field.////      USN_SOURCE_DATA_MANAGEMENT - Service is not modifying the external view//          of any part of the file.  Typical case is HSM moving data to//          and from external storage.////      USN_SOURCE_AUXILIARY_DATA - Service is not modifying the external view//          of the file with regard to the application that created this file.//          Can be used to add private data streams to a file.////      USN_SOURCE_REPLICATION_MANAGEMENT - Service is modifying a file to match//          the contents of the same file which exists in another member of the//          replica set.////      USN_SOURCE_CLIENT_REPLICATION_MANAGEMENT - Replication is being performed//          on client systems either from the cloud or servers.  A volume handle//          is not required to set this value//////  Flags for the HandleInfo field above:////  ------ Introduced in W2K://  MARK_HANDLE_PROTECT_CLUSTERS - disallow any defragmenting (FSCTL_MOVE_FILE) until the//      the handle is closed////  ------ Introduced in Vista://  MARK_HANDLE_TXF_SYSTEM_LOG - indicates that this stream is being used as the Txf//      log for an RM on the volume.  Must be called in the kernel using IRP_MN_KERNEL_CALL.////  MARK_HANDLE_NOT_TXF_SYSTEM_LOG - indicates that this component is no longer using this//      object as a TxF log file.////  ------ Introduced in Win7://  MARK_HANDLE_REALTIME - only supported by the UDFS file system.  Marks the device//      to do realtime streaming of video////  MARK_HANDLE_NOT_REALTIME - only supported by the UDFS file system.  Marks the device//      to no longer do realtime streaming of video////  MARK_HANDLE_CLOUD_SYNC - this flag is deprecated and is no longer used////  ------ Introduced in Win8//  MARK_HANDLE_READ_COPY - indicates the data must be read from the specified copy//      of data.  Only supported for spaces redundent volumes.////  MARK_HANDLE_NOT_READ_COPY - indicates the data is no longer to be read from a specific copy.////  ------ Introduced in WinBlue (win 8.1)//  MARK_HANDLE_FILTER_METADATA - Flag reserved for internal Microsoft use////  MARK_HANDLE_RETURN_PURGE_FAILURE - When intermixing memory mapped/cached IO with//      non-cached IO the system attempts, when a non-cached io is issued, to purge//      memory mappings for the range of the non-cached IO.  If these purges fail//      the system normally does not return the failure to the caller which can//      lead to corrupted state (which is why the documentation says to not do this).//      This flag tells the system to return purge failures for the given handle//      so the application can better handle this situation////  ------ Introduced in WinThreshold (win10)//  MARK_HANDLE_DISABLE_FILE_METADATA_OPTIMIZATION - This disabled the FRS compaction//      feature on the given file.////  MARK_HANDLE_ENABLE_USN_SOURCE_ON_PAGING_IO - Tells NTFS to set the given UsnSourceInfo//      value on Paging writes in the USN Journal.  Traditionally this was not//      done on paging writes since you did not know what thread made the given//      changes.  This is an override.  This only works of the FileObject MM is//      holding on to has this state associated with it.////  MARK_HANDLE_SKIP_COHERENCY_SYNC_DISALLOW_WRITES - Setting this flag tells//      the system that writes are not allowed on this file.  If someone tries//      to open the file for write access, the operation is failed with STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED.//      If a write is seen the operation is failed with STATUS_MARKED_TO_DISALLOW_WRITES////  ------ Introduced in RS4 (win10)//  MARK_HANDLE_ENABLE_CPU_CACHE - Flag reserved for internal Microsoft use////  ------ Introduced in Cobalt (win10)//  MARK_HANDLE_SUPPRESS_VOLUME_OPEN_FLUSH - Normally, on the first read/write operation//      on a volume handle (DASD open) the file system flushes the volume.  This can//      have performance consequences in certain scenarios.  If this flag is set on//      a volume handle it will suppress that flush on first IO.////#define ReservedForFutureUse                          (0x00000002)//#define ReservedForFutureUse                          (0x00000010)//                                                      (0x00000800)        // defined above: MARK_HANDLE_CLOUD_SYNC////==================== FSCTL_SECURITY_ID_CHECK ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_SECURITY_ID_CHECK//////==================== FSCTL_IS_VOLUME_DIRTY ======================////  Output flags for the FSCTL_IS_VOLUME_DIRTY is a DWORD//////==================== FSCTL_FILE_PREFETCH ======================//////==================== FSCTL_FILESYSTEM_GET_STATISTICS ======================//// Structures for FSCTL_FILESYSTEM_GET_STATISTICS//// Filesystem performance counters//// currently version 1// must by a multiple of 64 bytes////  The file system's private structure is appended here.//// values for FS_STATISTICS.FileSystemType////  File System Specific Statistics Data////// Other meta data io's////// Additions for NT 5.0//// number of individual calls to allocate clusters// number of clusters allocated// number of times a hint was specified// number of runs used to satisfy all the requests// number of times the hint was useful// number of clusters allocated via the hint// number of times the cache was useful other than the hint// number of clusters allocated via the cache other than the hint// number of times the cache wasn't useful// number of clusters allocated without the cache////  Additions for Windows 8.1//////  All future expansion of this structure needs to be in NTFS_STATISTICS_EX starting Windows 10//// currently version 1// must by a multiple of 64 bytes////  The file system's private structure is appended here.////// Other meta data io's////// Additions for NT 5.0//// number of individual calls to allocate clusters// number of runs used to satisfy all the requests// number of times a hint was specified// number of times the hint was useful// number of times the cache was useful other than the hint// number of times the cache wasn't useful// number of clusters allocated// number of clusters allocated via the hint// number of clusters allocated via the cache other than the hint// number of clusters allocated without the cache////  Additions for Windows 8.1//////  Additions for Windows 10//////  Additions for NtfsFillStatInfoFromMftRecord//////==================== FSCTL_SET_OBJECT_ID ================================//==================== FSCTL_GET_OBJECT_ID ================================//==================== FSCTL_CREATE_OR_GET_OBJECT_ID ======================////  Structures for FSCTL_SET_OBJECT_ID, FSCTL_GET_OBJECT_ID, and//  FSCTL_CREATE_OR_GET_OBJECT_ID//////  This is the portion of the object id that is indexed.//////  This portion of the object id is not indexed, it's just//  some metadata for the user's benefit.//////==================== FSCTL_SET_SPARSE ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_SET_SPARSE//////==================== FSCTL_SET_ZERO_DATA ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_SET_ZERO_DATA//////  When set tells the file system to not purge the cache for the given range//  being zeroed//////==================== FSCTL_QUERY_ALLOCATED_RANGES ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_QUERY_ALLOCATED_RANGES////// Querying the allocated ranges requires an output buffer to store the// allocated ranges and an input buffer to specify the range to query.// The input buffer contains a single entry, the output buffer is an// array of the following structure.//////====================== FSCTL_SET_ENCRYPTION ===============================//====================== FSCTL_WRITE_RAW_ENCRYPTED ==========================//====================== FSCTL_READ_RAW_ENCRYPTED ===========================//// Structures for FSCTL_SET_ENCRYPTION, FSCTL_WRITE_RAW_ENCRYPTED, and FSCTL_READ_RAW_ENCRYPTED//////  The input buffer to set encryption indicates whether we are to encrypt/decrypt a file//  or an individual stream.//////  The optional output buffer to set encryption indicates that the last encrypted//  stream in a file has been marked as decrypted.//////  Request Encrypted Data structure.  This is used to indicate//  the range of the file to read.  It also describes the//  output buffer used to return the data.//////  Requested file offset and requested length to read.//  The fsctl will round the starting offset down//  to a file system boundary.  It will also//  round the length up to a file system boundary.//////  Encrypted Data Information structure.  This structure//  is used to return raw encrypted data from a file in//  order to perform off-line recovery.  The data will be//  encrypted or encrypted and compressed.  The off-line//  service will need to use the encryption and compression//  format information to recover the file data.  In the//  event that the data is both encrypted and compressed then//  the decryption must occur before decompression.  All//  the data units below must be encrypted and compressed//  with the same format.////  The data will be returned in units.  The data unit size//  will be fixed per request.  If the data is compressed//  then the data unit size will be the compression unit size.////  This structure is at the beginning of the buffer used to//  return the encrypted data.  The actual raw bytes from//  the file will follow this buffer.  The offset of the//  raw bytes from the beginning of this structure is//  specified in the REQUEST_RAW_ENCRYPTED_DATA structure//  described above.//////  This is the file offset for the first entry in the//  data block array.  The file system will round//  the requested start offset down to a boundary//  that is consistent with the format of the file.//////  Data offset in output buffer.  The output buffer//  begins with an ENCRYPTED_DATA_INFO structure.//  The file system will then store the raw bytes from//  disk beginning at the following offset within the//  output buffer.//////  The number of bytes being returned that are within//  the size of the file.  If this value is less than//  (NumberOfDataBlocks << DataUnitShift), it means the//  end of the file occurs within this transfer.  Any//  data beyond file size is invalid and was never//  passed to the encryption driver.//////  The number of bytes being returned that are below//  valid data length.  If this value is less than//  (NumberOfDataBlocks << DataUnitShift), it means the//  end of the valid data occurs within this transfer.//  After decrypting the data from this transfer, any//  byte(s) beyond valid data length must be zeroed.//////  Code for the compression format as defined in//  ntrtl.h.  Note that COMPRESSION_FORMAT_NONE//  and COMPRESSION_FORMAT_DEFAULT are invalid if//  any of the described chunks are compressed.//////  The DataUnit is the granularity used to access the//  disk.  It will be the same as the compression unit//  size for a compressed file.  For an uncompressed//  file, it will be some cluster-aligned power of 2 that//  the file system deems convenient.  A caller should//  not expect that successive calls will have the//  same data unit shift value as the previous call.////  Since chunks and compression units are expected to be//  powers of 2 in size, we express them log2.  So, for//  example (1 << ChunkShift) == ChunkSizeInBytes.  The//  ClusterShift indicates how much space must be saved//  to successfully compress a compression unit - each//  successfully compressed data unit must occupy//  at least one cluster less in bytes than an uncompressed//  data block unit.//////  The format for the encryption.//////  This is the number of entries in the data block size//  array.//////  This is an array of sizes in the data block array.  There//  must be one entry in this array for each data block//  read from disk.  The size has a different meaning//  depending on whether the file is compressed.////  A size of zero always indicates that the final data consists entirely//  of zeroes.  There is no decryption or decompression to//  perform.////  If the file is compressed then the data block size indicates//  whether this block is compressed.  A size equal to//  the block size indicates that the corresponding block did//  not compress.  Any other non-zero size indicates the//  size of the compressed data which needs to be//  decrypted/decompressed.////  If the file is not compressed then the data block size//  indicates the amount of data within the block that//  needs to be decrypted.  Any other non-zero size indicates//  that the remaining bytes in the data unit within the file//  consists of zeros.  An example of this is when the//  the read spans the valid data length of the file.  There//  is no data to decrypt past the valid data length.//////  Extended encryption structure for read/write raw encrypted operations.//  This was needed so we can explicitly indicate if a file is sparse or not////  Flag to indicate the encrypted file is sparse//////  This encrypted file data is from a filesystem which has support//  for sparse data ranges (e.g. ReFS) for non-sparse files.//////  This encrypted file data is for a sparse file from a filesystem//  which has a 4k sparse data unit size.  It cannot be restored to//  a filesystem with larger sparse unit size.//////  This is really a 4 byte character array which//  must have the value "EXTD".  We use this//  to determine if we should read the extended data//  or not.//////  The length of the extended data structure//////  Encrypted data flags//////======================== FSCTL_READ_FROM_PLEX ===========================////  Request Plex Read Data structure.  This is used to indicate//  the range of the file to read.  It also describes//  which plex to perform the read from.//////  Requested offset and length to read.//  The offset can be the virtual offset (vbo) in to a file,//  or a volume. In the case of a file offset,//  the fsd will round the starting offset down//  to a file system boundary.  It will also//  round the length up to a file system boundary and//  enforce any other applicable limits.//////======================== FSCTL_SIS_COPYFILE ===========================//// Source and destination file names are passed in the FileNameBuffer.// Both strings are null terminated, with the source name starting at// the beginning of FileNameBuffer, and the destination name immediately// following.  Length fields include terminating nulls.//////======================== FSCTL_MAKE_MEDIA_COMPATIBLE ===========================////  Input parameter structure for FSCTL_MAKE_MEDIA_COMPATIBLE//////======================== FSCTL_SET_DEFECT_MANAGEMENT ===========================////  Input parameter structure for FSCTL_SET_DEFECT_MANAGEMENT//////======================== FSCTL_QUERY_SPARING_INFO ===========================////  Output structure for FSCTL_QUERY_SPARING_INFO//////===================== FSCTL_QUERY_ON_DISK_VOLUME_INFO ========================////  Output structure for FSCTL_QUERY_ON_DISK_VOLUME_INFO//// -1 = unknown// -1 = unknown// -1 = unknown or n/a// -1 = unknown or n/a////===================== FSCTL_SET_REPAIR ========================////  Input flags for FSCTL_SET_REPAIR//////===================== FSCTL_INITIATE_REPAIR ========================////  Optional output structure for FSCTL_INITIATE_REPAIR//////  We need to expose CLSN definition???????????????//////===================== FSCTL_SHRINK_VOLUME ========================////  Input structures for FSCTL_SHRINK_VOLUME.//////========= FSCTL_TXFS_MODIFY_RM,  FSCTL_TXFS_QUERY_RM_INFORMATION ============////  Structures for FSCTL_TXFS_MODIFY_RM and FSCTL_TXFS_QUERY_RM_INFORMATION////  For ModifyRM, TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_GROWTH_INCREMENT_NUM_CONTAINERS and//  TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_GROWTH_INCREMENT_PERCENT are mutually exclusive.//  You can specify the log growth amount in number of containers or as a percentage.////  For ModifyRM, TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_CONTAINER_COUNT_MAX and//  TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_NO_CONTAINER_COUNT_MAX are mutually exclusive.////  For ModifyRM, TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_CONTAINER_COUNT_MIN and//  TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_NO_CONTAINER_COUNT_MIN are mutually exclusive.////  For ModifyRM, TXFS_RM_FLAG_RESET_RM_AT_NEXT_START and//  TXFS_RM_FLAG_DO_NOT_RESET_RM_AT_NEXT_START are mutually exclusive and only//  apply to default RMs.////  For ModifyRM, TXFS_RM_FLAG_PREFER_CONSISTENCY and//  TXFS_RM_FLAG_PREFER_AVAILABILITY are mutually exclusive.  After calling ModifyRM//  with one of these flags set the RM must be restarted for the change to take effect.//////  TXFS_RM_FLAG_* flags//////  Maximum log container count if TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_CONTAINER_COUNT_MAX is set.//////  Minimum log container count if TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_CONTAINER_COUNT_MIN is set.//////  Target log container count for TXFS_RM_FLAG_SHRINK_LOG or _GROW_LOG.//////  When the log is full, increase its size by this much.  Indicated as either a percent of//  the log size or absolute container count, depending on which of the TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_GROWTH_INCREMENT_*//  flags is set.//////  Sets autoshrink policy if TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_AUTO_SHRINK_PERCENTAGE is set.  Autoshrink//  makes the log shrink so that no more than this percentage of the log is free at any time.//////  Reserved.//////  If TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOGGING_MODE is set, this must contain one of TXFS_LOGGING_MODE_SIMPLE//  or TXFS_LOGGING_MODE_FULL.//////  The flags field for query RM information is used for the following information:////  1)  To indicate whether the LogGrowthIncrement field is reported as a percent//      or as a number of containers.  Possible flag values for this are:////      TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_GROWTH_INCREMENT_NUM_CONTAINERS xor TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_GROWTH_INCREMENT_PERCENT////  2)  To indicate that there is no set maximum or minimum container count.  Possible//      flag values for this are:////      TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_NO_CONTAINER_COUNT_MAX//      TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_NO_CONTAINER_COUNT_MIN////      Note that these flags are not mutually exclusive.////  2)  To report whether the RM will be reset the next time it is started.  Note that//      only the default RM will report a meaningful value (secondary RMs will always//      report DO_NOT_RESET) Possible flag values for this are:////      TXFS_RM_FLAG_RESET_RM_AT_NEXT_START xor TXFS_RM_FLAG_DO_NOT_RESET_RM_AT_NEXT_START////  3)  To report whether the RM is in consistency mode or availability mode.  Possible//      flag values for this are:////      TXFS_RM_FLAG_PREFER_CONSISTENCY xor TXFS_RM_FLAG_PREFER_AVAILABILITY////  The RmState field can have exactly one of the above-defined TXF_RM_STATE_ values.//////  If the return value is STATUS_BUFFER_OVERFLOW (ERROR_MORE_DATA), this//  will indicate how much space is required to hold everything.//////  LSN of earliest available record in the RM's log.//////  LSN of most recently-written record in the RM's log.//////  LSN of the log's archive tail.//////  Size of a log container in bytes.//////  Highest virtual clock value recorded in this RM's log.//////  Number of containers in this RM's log.//////  Maximum-allowed log container count.//////  Minimum-allowed log container count.//////  Amount by which log will grow when it gets full.  Indicated as either a percent of//  the log size or absolute container count, depending on which of the TXFS_RM_FLAG_LOG_GROWTH_INCREMENT_*//  flags is set.//////  Reports on the autoshrink policy if.  Autoshrink makes the log shrink so that no more than this//  percentage of the log is free at any time.  A value of 0 indicates that autoshrink is off (i.e.//  the log will not automatically shrink).//////  TXFS_RM_FLAG_* flags.  See the comment above at TXFS_QUERY_RM_INFORMATION_VALID_FLAGS to see//  what the flags here mean.//////  Exactly one of TXFS_LOGGING_MODE_SIMPLE or TXFS_LOGGING_MODE_FULL.//////  Reserved.//////  Activity state of the RM.  May be exactly one of the above-defined TXF_RM_STATE_ values.//////  Total capacity of the log in bytes.//////  Amount of free space in the log in bytes.//////  Size of $Tops in bytes.//////  Amount of space in $Tops in use.//////  Number of transactions active in the RM at the time of the call.//////  Total number of single-phase commits that have happened the RM.//////  Total number of two-phase commits that have happened the RM.//////  Number of times the log has filled up.//////  Age of oldest active transaction in the RM, in milliseconds.//////  Name of the RM.//////  Offset in bytes from the beginning of this structure to a NULL-terminated Unicode//  string indicating the path to the RM's transaction manager's log.//////======================== FSCTL_TXFS_ROLLFORWARD_REDO ========================//// Structures for FSCTL_TXFS_ROLLFORWARD_REDO//////======================== FSCTL_TXFS_START_RM ========================////  Structures for FSCTL_TXFS_START_RM////  Note that TXFS_START_RM_FLAG_LOG_GROWTH_INCREMENT_NUM_CONTAINERS and//  TXFS_START_RM_FLAG_LOG_GROWTH_INCREMENT_PERCENT are mutually exclusive.//  You can specify the log growth amount in number of containers or as a percentage.////  TXFS_START_RM_FLAG_CONTAINER_COUNT_MAX and TXFS_START_RM_FLAG_LOG_NO_CONTAINER_COUNT_MAX//  are mutually exclusive.////  TXFS_START_RM_FLAG_LOG_CONTAINER_COUNT_MIN and TXFS_START_RM_FLAG_LOG_NO_CONTAINER_COUNT_MIN//  are mutually exclusive.////  TXFS_START_RM_FLAG_PREFER_CONSISTENCY and TXFS_START_RM_FLAG_PREFER_AVAILABILITY//  are mutually exclusive.////  Optional parameters will have system-supplied defaults applied if omitted.//////  TXFS_START_RM_FLAG_* flags.//////  RM log container size, in bytes.  This parameter is optional.//////  RM minimum log container count.  This parameter is optional.//////  RM maximum log container count.  This parameter is optional.//////  RM log growth increment in number of containers or percent, as indicated//  by TXFS_START_RM_FLAG_LOG_GROWTH_INCREMENT_* flag.  This parameter is//  optional.//////  RM log auto shrink percentage.  This parameter is optional.//////  Offset from the beginning of this structure to the log path for the KTM//  instance to be used by this RM.  This must be a two-byte (WCHAR) aligned//  value.  This parameter is required.//////  Length in bytes of log path for the KTM instance to be used by this RM.//  This parameter is required.//////  Logging mode for this RM.  One of TXFS_LOGGING_MODE_SIMPLE or//  TXFS_LOGGING_MODE_FULL (mutually exclusive).  This parameter is optional,//  and will default to TXFS_LOGGING_MODE_SIMPLE.//////  Length in bytes of the path to the log to be used by the RM.  This parameter//  is required.//////  Reserved.//////  The path to the log (in Unicode characters) to be used by the RM goes here.//  This parameter is required.//////======================== FSCTL_TXFS_GET_METADATA_INFO ========================////  Structures for FSCTL_TXFS_GET_METADATA_INFO//////  Returns the TxfId of the file referenced by the handle used to call this routine.//////  The GUID of the transaction that has the file locked, if applicable.//////  Returns the LSN for the most recent log record we've written for the file.//////  Transaction state, a TXFS_TRANSACTION_STATE_* value.//////================= FSCTL_TXFS_LIST_TRANSACTION_LOCKED_FILES ==================////  Structures for FSCTL_TXFS_LIST_TRANSACTION_LOCKED_FILES////  TXFS_LIST_TRANSACTION_LOCKED_FILES_ENTRY_FLAG_CREATED means the reported name was created//  in the locking transaction.////  TXFS_LIST_TRANSACTION_LOCKED_FILES_ENTRY_FLAG_DELETED means the reported name was deleted//  in the locking transaction.////  Note that both flags may appear if the name was both created and deleted in the same//  transaction.  In that case the FileName[] member will contain only "\0", as there is//  no meaningful name to report.//////  Offset in bytes from the beginning of the TXFS_LIST_TRANSACTION_LOCKED_FILES//  structure to the next TXFS_LIST_TRANSACTION_LOCKED_FILES_ENTRY.//////  TXFS_LIST_TRANSACTION_LOCKED_FILES_ENTRY_FLAG_* flags to indicate whether the//  current name was deleted or created in the transaction.//////  NTFS File ID of the file.//////  Reserved.//////  NULL-terminated Unicode path to this file, relative to RM root.//////  GUID name of the KTM transaction that files should be enumerated from.//////  On output, the number of files involved in the transaction on this RM.//////  The length of the buffer required to obtain the complete list of files.//  This value may change from call to call as the transaction locks more files.//////  Offset in bytes from the beginning of this structure to the first//  TXFS_LIST_TRANSACTION_LOCKED_FILES_ENTRY.//////==================== FSCTL_TXFS_LIST_TRANSACTIONS ======================////  Structures for FSCTL_TXFS_LIST_TRANSACTIONS//////  Transaction GUID.//////  Transaction state, a TXFS_TRANSACTION_STATE_* value.//////  Reserved fields//////  On output, the number of transactions involved in this RM.//////  The length of the buffer required to obtain the complete list of//  transactions.  Note that this value may change from call to call//  as transactions enter and exit the system.//////==================== FSCTL_TXFS_READ_BACKUP_INFORMATION ======================////  Structures for FSCTL_TXFS_READ_BACKUP_INFORMATION//////  Used to return the required buffer size if return code is STATUS_BUFFER_OVERFLOW//////  On success the data is copied here.//////==================== FSCTL_TXFS_WRITE_BACKUP_INFORMATION ======================////  Structures for FSCTL_TXFS_WRITE_BACKUP_INFORMATION//////  The data returned in the Buffer member of a previous call to//  FSCTL_TXFS_READ_BACKUP_INFORMATION goes here.//////==================== FSCTL_TXFS_GET_TRANSACTED_VERSION ======================////  Output structure for FSCTL_TXFS_GET_TRANSACTED_VERSION//////  The version that this handle is opened to.  This will be//  TXFS_TRANSACTED_VERSION_UNCOMMITTED for nontransacted and//  transactional writer handles.//////  The most recent committed version available.//////  If this is a handle to a miniversion, the ID of the miniversion.//  If it is not a handle to a miniversion, this field will be 0.//////  The first available miniversion.  Unless the miniversions are//  visible to the transaction bound to this handle, this field will be zero.//////  The latest available miniversion.  Unless the miniversions are//  visible to the transaction bound to this handle, this field will be zero.//////==================== FSCTL_TXFS_SAVEPOINT_INFORMATION ======================////  Structures for FSCTL_TXFS_SAVEPOINT_INFORMATION////  Note that the TXFS_SAVEPOINT_INFORMATION structure is both and in and out structure.//  The KtmTransaction and ActionCode members are always in-parameters, and the SavepointId//  member is either an in-parameter, an out-parameter, or not used (see its definition below).//////  Create a new savepoint.//////  Roll back to a specified savepoint.//////  Clear (make unavailable for rollback) the most recently set savepoint//  that has not yet been cleared.//////  Clear all savepoints from the transaction.//////  Handle to the transaction on which to perform the savepoint operation.//////  Specifies the savepoint action to take.  A TXFS_SAVEPOINT_* value.//////  In-parameter for TXFS_ROLLBACK_TO_SAVEPOINT - specifies the savepoint to which//  to roll back.////  Out-parameter for TXFS_SET_SAVEPOINT - the newly-created savepoint ID will be//  returned here.////  Not used for TXFS_CLEAR_SAVEPOINT or TXFS_CLEAR_ALL_SAVEPOINTS.//////==================== FSCTL_TXFS_CREATE_MINIVERSION ======================////  Structures for FSCTL_TXFS_CREATE_MINIVERSION////      Only an out parameter is necessary.  That returns the identifier of the new miniversion created.//////  The base version for the newly created miniversion.//////  The miniversion that was just created.//////==================== FSCTL_TXFS_TRANSACTION_ACTIVE ======================////  Structure for FSCTL_TXFS_TRANSACTION_ACTIVE//////  Whether or not the volume had active transactions when this snapshot was taken.//////======================= FSCTL_GET_BOOT_AREA_INFO ==========================//// Output structure for FSCTL_GET_BOOT_AREA_INFO//// the count of boot sectors present on the file system// variable number of boot sectors.////==================== FSCTL_GET_RETRIEVAL_POINTER_BASE ======================//// Output structure for FSCTL_GET_RETRIEVAL_POINTER_BASE//// sector offset to the first allocatable unit on the filesystem////==================== FSCTL_QUERY_PERSISTENT_VOLUME_STATE ====================//==================== FSCTL_SET_PERSISTENT_VOLUME_STATE ======================//// Structure for FSCTL_SET_PERSISTENT_VOLUME_STATE and//  FSCTL_GET_PERSISTENT_VOLUME_STATE.  The initial version will be 1.0//////  VolumeFlags values//////  Persistent volume flags to control the file system's storage tiering//  awareness.//////  These are flags that define a volume's dependency on WimBoot file.//////  The volume backs a system critical volume, typically the one that has wimboot//  image file serving the system files//////  The volume is backed by other volume that actually has the system files.//  And hence this relies on the other volume being present in order for the system to boot up.//////  Writes dynamic redirection on tiered volumes will be disabled//////  Disable txf on volume//////  Always reallocate data writes//////  This indicates that AutoChk modified this volume and is cleared by AutoChk//  after it ensured that the volume is dismounted or the system is rebooted.//  This can be set or queried.//////  This again indicates that AutoChk modified this volume but is cleared by//  NTFS on next mount.  So if this flag is set it means that the volume was//  modified by AutoChk while it's still mounted and the on-disk state and//  the in memory state could be different.  This can only be queried.//////  The volume was formatted as DAX.  The volume may not be mounted as DAX//  if the storage is not DAX capable.  This can only be queried.//////  The volume was formatted as a developer volume.  This can be used by the//  file system and other system components to enable optimizations that doe//  not require an administrator to trust the volume on a given machine.////  This is set at format time and can only be queried.//////  An adminstrator on a given machine had trust this volume and is willing//  to enable optimizations like not having anti-virus filters attach to the//  volume.  This information is persisted in the registry on a given machine.//  This can be used by the file system and other system components to enable//  optimizations that require an administrator to trust the volume on a given//  machine.////  NOTE: Today only developer volumes can be trusted, i.e. this flag can be//  set only when PERSISTENT_VOLUME_STATE_DEV_VOLUME is set.////  This can be set and queried.//////==================== FSCTL_QUERY_FILE_SYSTEM_RECOGNITION ====================////  Structure for FSCTL_QUERY_FILE_SYSTEM_RECOGNITION//////=========================== FSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK ===========================////  Structures for FSCTL_REQUEST_OPLOCK//////  This should be set to REQUEST_OPLOCK_CURRENT_VERSION.//////  One or more OPLOCK_LEVEL_CACHE_* values to indicate the desired level of the oplock.//////  REQUEST_OPLOCK_INPUT_FLAG_* flags.//// If the oplock request fails with STATUS_OPLOCK_NOT_GRANTED, this flag indicates that the oplock// could not be granted due to the presence of a writable user-mapped section.////  This should be set to REQUEST_OPLOCK_CURRENT_VERSION.//////  One or more OPLOCK_LEVEL_CACHE_* values indicating the level of the oplock that//  was just broken.//////  One or more OPLOCK_LEVEL_CACHE_* values indicating the level to which an oplock//  is being broken, or an oplock level that may be available for granting, depending//  on the operation returning this buffer.//////  REQUEST_OPLOCK_OUTPUT_FLAG_* flags.//////  When REQUEST_OPLOCK_OUTPUT_FLAG_MODES_PROVIDED is set, and when the//  OPLOCK_LEVEL_CACHE_HANDLE level is being lost in an oplock break, these fields//  contain the access mode and share mode of the request that is causing the break.//////======================= FSCTL_QUERY_DEPENDENT_VOLUME =======================//////  These structures are used by the FSCTL_QUERY_DEPENDENT_VOLUME//// Root parent is 0, every child level after that is incremented////========================== FSCTL_SD_GLOBAL_CHANGE ==========================////  Structures for FSCTL_SD_GLOBAL_CHANGE//////  list of operations supported//////  Operation specific structures for SD_GLOBAL_CHANGE_TYPE_MACHINE_SID//////  The current machine SID to change.//  This defines the offset from the beginning of the SD_GLOBAL_CHANGE_INPUT//  structure of where the CurrentMachineSID to replace begins.  This will//  be a SID structure.  The length defines the length of the imbedded SID//  structure.//////  The new machine SID value to set in-place of the current machine SID//  This defines the offset from the beginning of the SD_GLOBAL_CHANGE_INPUT//  structure of where the NewMachineSID to set begins.  This will//  be a SID structure.  The length defines the length of the imbedded SID//  structure.//////  How many entries were successfully changed in the $Secure stream//////  How many entries failed the update in the $Secure stream//////  How many entries are unused in the current security stream//////  The total number of entries processed in the $Secure stream//////  How many entries were successfully changed in the $MFT file//////  How many entries failed the update in the $MFT file//////  Total number of entries processed in the $MFT file//////  Operation specific structures for SD_GLOBAL_CHANGE_TYPE_QUERY_STATS//////  Stream size and allocation size for the security descriptor//  data stream ($Secure:$SDS).//////  Stream size and allocation size for the security ID index//  stream ($Secure:$SII).//////  Stream size and allocation size for the security descriptor//  hash index stream ($Secure:$SDH).//////  The total number of entries in the security descriptor data//  stream.//////  The number of unused entries in the security descriptor data//  stream.//////  Operation specific structures for SD_GLOBAL_CHANGE_TYPE_ENUM_SDS//////  The byte offset within the security descriptor data stream to look//  for security descriptors.  This must be a multiple of 16.////  Note this is a virtual byte offset.  The security descriptor data//  stream contains some additional data embedded inside the stream for//  redundancy, and this extra data does not count toward this byte//  offset.//////  The maximum number of SD entries to return.  This is primarily//  useful for supporting a model where you only want one entry at//  a time.  Set to 0 for no max.//////  Hash value of the security decriptor.//////  SecurityId for the security descriptor.//////  Virtual byte offset of this header in the security descriptor//  data stream.//////  Length of this header + security descriptor that follows this//  header.//////  Actual security decriptor, variable length.//////  The current machine SID to change.//  This defines the offset from the beginning of the SD_GLOBAL_CHANGE_INPUT//  structure of where the CurrentMachineSID to replace begins.  This will//  be a SID structure.  The length defines the length of the imbedded SID//  structure.//////  The total number of entries returned.//////  The total number of bytes of entries returned.//////  Variable length array of security descriptor stream entries.//  There should be NumSDEntriesReturned of them.  To walk this array,//  first point to SDEntry[0], then advance a number of bytes equal//  to the entry's Length rounded up to a multiple of 16.  (Each entry//  will be aligned to a 16-byte boundary.)//////  Generic INPUT & OUTPUT structures for FSCTL_SD_GLOBAL_CHANGE//////  Input flags (none currently defined)//////  Specifies which type of change we are doing and pics which member//  of the below union is in use.//////  Output State Flags (none currently defined)//////  Specifies which below union to use//////==================== FSCTL_LOOKUP_STREAM_FROM_CLUSTER =======================//////  Flags for the operation.  Currently no flags are defined.//////  Number of clusters in the following array of clusters.//  The input buffer must be large enough to contain this//  number or the operation will fail.//////  An array of one or more clusters to look up.//////  Offset from the beginning of this structure to the first entry//  returned.  If no entries are returned, this value is zero.//////  Number of matches to the input criteria.  Note that more matches//  may be found than entries returned if the buffer is not large//  enough.//////  Minimum size of the buffer, in bytes, which would be needed to//  contain all matching entries to the input criteria.//////  Offset from the beginning of this structure to the next entry//  returned.  If there are no more entries, this value is zero.//////  Flags describing characteristics about this stream.//////  This value is reserved and is currently zero.//////  This is the cluster that this entry refers to.  It will be one//  of the clusters passed in the input structure.//////  A NULL-terminated Unicode string containing the path of the//  object relative to the root of the volume.  This string//  will refer to the attribute or stream represented by the//  cluster.//////==================== FSCTL_FILE_TYPE_NOTIFICATION =======================////  This is the structure for the FSCTL_FILE_TYPE_NOTIFICATION operation.//  Its purpose is to notify the storage stack about the extents of certain//  types of files.  This is only callable from kernel mode//////  Flags for this operation//  FILE_TYPE_NOTIFICATION_FLAG_*//////  A count of how many FileTypeID guids are given//////  This is a unique identifier for the type of file notification occurring//////  Flags for the given operation//////  These are the globally defined file types//////=========================FSCTL_CSV_MGMT_LOCK===========================//////========================= FSCTL_IS_CSV_FILE ============================//// Structure for FSCTL_IS_CSV_FILE//////========================= FSCTL_CSV_CONTROL =============================////// Output for the CsvControlQueryRedirectState////// Output for the CsvControlQueryFileRevision// Note that revision tracking is per file, and not per// stream so it changes every time one of the stream// changes.////// NTFS File Id////// FileRevision[0] increases each time the CSV MDS// stack is rebuilt and CSVFLT loses its state.//// FileRevision[1] increases each time CSV MDS// stack purges the cached revision # for the// file.//// FileRevision[2] increases each time CSV MDS// observes that file sizes might have// changed or the file might have been written// to. It also is incremented when one of the// nodes performs the first direct IO on a stream// associated with this file after opening this stream.//// If any of the numbers are 0 the caller should// assume that the file was modified////// Output for the CsvControlQueryFileRevisionFileId128// Note that revision tracking is per file, and not per// stream so it changes every time one of the stream// changes.////// File Id////// FileRevision[0] increases each time the CSV MDS// stack is rebuilt and CSVFLT loses its state.//// FileRevision[1] increases each time CSV MDS// stack purges the cached revision # for the// file.//// FileRevision[2] increases each time CSV MDS// observes that file sizes might have// changed or the file might have been written// to. It also is incremented when one of the// nodes performs the first direct IO on a stream// associated with this file after opening this stream.//// If any of the numbers are 0 the caller should// assume that the file was modified////// Output for the CsvControlQueryMdsPath// This control returns the path that is used by CSV to// communicate to the MDS////// Output for the CsvControlQueryVolumeRedirectState////// Structure is defined up to and including field PathLength////// Output for the CsvControlGetCsvFsMdsPathV2////// Version of the structure.// CSV_QUERY_MDS_PATH_V2_VERSION_*////// Required output buffer size to completely fit// all the data////// Id of MDS and of the current node.// When these two Ids are the same then current node is MDS// otherwise current node is a DS////// A combination of CSV_QUERY_MDS_PATH_FLAG_* flags////// Describes disk connectivity across all cluster nodes////// Volume GUID of this CSV volume////// Offset to an array of DIPs of MDS and number of bytes// in that array. Each array entry has type SOCKADDR_INET// number of elements is IpAddressLength / sizeof(SOCKADDR_INET)////// Offset to the a Unicode string that contains// path this node is using to open files on MDS, and number of// characters in that string////// Input for the CsvControlSetVolumeId////// Output for the CsvControlQueryVolumeId//////========================= FSCTL_LMR_QUERY_INFO =============================////// Output for the LMRQuerySessionInfo//////====================== FSCTL_CSV_QUERY_VETO_FILE_DIRECT_IO =========================//// In output buffer set Veto to TRUE to prevent CsvFs from// performing DirectIO on the stream.// VetoedFromAltitude and Reason are optional fields that// are used to help with troubleshooting. CSV writes these// strings to the diagnostic log. Filter can set it to a// string that would hint why volume is in a redirected mode.////// Storage Reserve common definitions//////========================= FSCTL_IS_VOLUME_OWNED_BYCSVFS ============================//////======================== FSCTL_FILE_LEVEL_TRIM ===========================////  Structure definitions for supporting file level trim//////  Bytes offset from the front of the given file to trim at//////  Length in bytes to trim from the given offset//////  Input buffer defining what ranges to trim////// Used when interacting with byte range locks. Set to zero if not SMB or//  similar.////// A count of how many Offset:Length pairs are given//////  All the pairs.//////  This is an optional output buffer//////  Receives the number of input ranges//  that were processed//////==================== FSCTL_QUERY_FILE_LAYOUT ===========================////// Clear the state of the internal cursor.//////  Request that the API call retrieve name information for the//  objects on the volume.//////  Request that the API call include streams of the file.//////  Include extent information with the attribute entries, where applicable.//  Use of this flag requires the _INCLUDE_STREAMS flag.//////  Include extra information, such as modification times and security//  IDs, with each returned file layout entry.//////  Include unallocated attributes in the enumeration, which in NTFS means one//  of three cases://      1. Resident attributes.//      2. Nonresident attributes of length 0.//      3. Compressed or sparse nonresident attributes with no physical//         allocation (consisting only of a sparse hole).//  This flag may only be used when no cluster ranges are specified (i.e.//  on a whole-volume query).//////  Request the full path to the file be included in the file name.//  This flag must be used with QUERY_FILE_LAYOUT_INCLUDE_NAMES//////  Enable QueryFileLayout to include information on attribute streams.//  Additionally, individual stream information flags must be enabled for//  information on a given stream to be returned.//////  Have QueryFileLayout include information (defined by DesiredStorageClass in StreamInformation)//  on DSC streams.//  This flag must be used in conjunction with QUERY_FILE_LAYOUT_INCLUDE_STREAM_INFORMATION//////  Have QueryFileLayout include information on TxF streams.//  This flag must be used in conjunction with QUERY_FILE_LAYOUT_INCLUDE_STREAM_INFORMATION//////  Have QueryFileLayout include information on EFS streams.//  This flag must be used in conjunction with QUERY_FILE_LAYOUT_INCLUDE_STREAM_INFORMATION//////  We can ask (politely) QueryFileLayout to only return files that have//  a given set of attributes present. This flag must be used with at least//  one attribute type flag or Query File Layout will return no files.//////  Have QueryFileLayout include files with a DSC attribute in the output buffer.//  This must be used in conjunction with QUERY_FILE_LAYOUT_INCLUDE_ONLY_FILES_WITH_SPECIFIC_ATTRIBUTES//////  Have QueryFileLayout include information (defined by DataStream in StreamInformation) on $DATA streams.//  This flag must be used in conjunction with QUERY_FILE_LAYOUT_INCLUDE_STREAM_INFORMATION//////  Have QueryFileLayout include information (defined by Reparse in StreamInformation) on $REPARSE_POINT streams.//  This flag must be used in conjunction with QUERY_FILE_LAYOUT_INCLUDE_STREAM_INFORMATION//////  Have QueryFileLayout include information (defined by Ea as in StreamInformation) on $EA streams.//  This flag must be used in conjunction with QUERY_FILE_LAYOUT_INCLUDE_STREAM_INFORMATION////// The starting cluster for this query region// (inclusive).////// The length of the cluster region.////// The starting file reference number for this// query region (inclusive).////// The ending file reference number for this// query region (inclusive).////// Number of filter entries in the following array.// The input buffer must be large enough to contain this// number or the operation will fail.//// This was originally named NumberOfPairs when there// was only one type of filter.  The union is simply to// maintain code compatibility.////// Flags for the operation.//////  The type of filter being applied for this operation.//////  Reserved for future use. Should be set to zero.//////  A pointer to the filter-type-specific information.  This is//  the caller's actual set of cluster ranges, etc.//////  The following is used when the caller wishes to filter//  on a set of cluster ranges.//////  The following is used when the caller wishes to filter//  on a set of file reference ranges.//////  The following is used when the caller wishes to filter//  on a set of storage reserve IDs.////// Indicates that the filesystem is returning stream extents in a// single-instanced fashion.////// Number of file entries following this header.// Includes only the number of file entries in// this iteration.////// Offset to the first file entry in this buffer,// expressed in bytes.////// Flags indicating context that is applicable to the// entire output set.////// For alignment/later use.////// Version number of this structure// (current version number is 1).////// Offset to next file entry (in bytes)// or zero if this is the last entry.////// Flags containing context applicable to this// file.////// File attributes.////// File ID for this file.////// Offset to the first name entry// from the start of this record, or// zero if there are no link records.////// Offset to the first stream entry// from the start of this record, or// zero if there are no stream records.////// Offset to additional per-file information,// contained in a FILE_LAYOUT_INFO_ENTRY// structure, or zero if this information was// not returned.////// Number of bytes accessible in additional per-file// information, contained in a FILE_LAYOUT_INFO_ENTRY// structure, or zero if this information was// not returned.//// Since pre-RS5 this was a reserved field that was// always set to zero by the file system, if// ExtraInfoOffset is non-zero but ExtraInfoLength// is zero then callers can assume the extra info// includes all fields up to Usn.  If ExtraInfoLength// is non-zero then it should be used by callers to// determine which fields are safe to access.////// The structure may be extended here to support// additional static fields (e.g. pointing to// a FILE_BASIC_INFORMATION structure, etc.). This// sort of change should coincide with a version// number increase.//// Each file name entry may be one, both, or neither of// these.////// Offset to next name entry (in bytes)// or zero if this is the last entry.////// Flags for this file name entry.////// Parent FRN for this link.////// File name length (bytes).////// For later use/alignment.////// Starting point for the name itself// (NOT null-terminated).////// Basic information for this file.////// Owner ID for this file.////// Security ID for this file.////// Update sequence number for this file.////// Storage Reserve ID assigned to the file (0 for none).////// This attribute/stream is known to the filesystem to be immovable.////// This attribute/stream is currently pinned by another application.// It is unmovable for the duration of the pin.////// This attribute is resident.////// This attribute has no clusters allocated to it.////// This layout entry contains the information (data) for the attribute////// Version of this struct. Current version is 2.////// Offset to the next stream entry (bytes).////// FSCTL-specific flags.////// Offset to the extent information buffer// for this stream, or zero if none exists.// This is relative to the start of this// stream record.////// Total allocated size of this stream,// in bytes.////// End of file location as a byte offset.//////  Offset to stream information. This is the//  content of the stream////// Attribute code.////// Stream attribute flags.////// Length of the stream identifier, in bytes.////// Starting point for the stream identifier// buffer.////// Flag noting that the extent information may be interpreted as// a RETRIEVAL_POINTERS_BUFFER structure////// Flag noting that all of the stream's extents are returned in// this structure, even if only some of them fall within the caller's// specified interest region(s).////// Extent-level flags for this entry.////// All that's defined for now is a retrieval// pointers buffer, since this is what NTFS// will use.//////==================== FSCTL_GET_INTEGRITY_INFORMATION / FSCTL_SET_INTEGRITY_INFORMATION ===========================//// Checksum algorithm. e.g. CHECKSUM_TYPE_UNCHANGED, CHECKSUM_TYPE_NONE, CHECKSUM_TYPE_CRC32// Must be 0// FSCTL_INTEGRITY_FLAG_xxx// Checksum algorithm. e.g. CHECKSUM_TYPE_UNCHANGED, CHECKSUM_TYPE_NONE, CHECKSUM_TYPE_CRC32// Must be 0// FSCTL_INTEGRITY_FLAG_xxx////==================== FSCTL_SET_INTEGRITY_INFORMATION_EX ===========================//// 0 to disable integrity, 1 to enable integrity// 1 to keep the current integrity state and ignore the value in EnableIntegrity, 0 to use EnableIntegrity value// Must be 0// FSCTL_INTEGRITY_FLAG_xxx// Structure version info, must be 1// Must be 0////======================== FSCTL_OFFLOAD_READ ==============================////  Structures used by FSCTL_OFFLOAD_READ operation//// In milliseconds////======================== FSCTL_OFFLOAD_WRITE =============================////  Structures used by FSCTL_OFFLOAD_WRITE operation//////======================== FSCTL_SET_PURGE_FAILURE_MODE ===========================////  Structure definitions for supporting purge failure mode//////======================= FSCTL_REPAIR_COPIES =============================//// sizeof(REPAIR_COPIES_INPUT)// Reserved (must be zero)// The copy number of the source copy.// The number of copies that will be repaired.// The copy numbers of all the copies that will be repaired.// sizeof(REPAIR_COPIES_OUTPUT)// Operational status// File Offset hint to use to resume repair operation skipping// the range where errors were found (operational Status is non-success).//======================= FSCTL_QUERY_FILE_REGIONS =============================////  Structures used for querying for Various file region definitions//  The initial values if for query VDL//////  Bit flags which may be OR'd together to define the usage of the given range//      If the given bit is SET, then the specified region has that attribute//      if the bit is NOT set, then the specified region does NOT have that attribute////none currently defined//count of total regions that could be returned//count of regions that were returned//for longlong alignment////  This is an optional buffer given on input to specify a region of the//  file the caller is interested in////======================= FSCTL_WRITE_USN_REASON =============================////  The list of valid USN reasons that can be set with this FSCTL.  Any bits//  which are specified which are not defined by this mask are silently//  stripped.  This operation will not be failed for sending undefined reasons//  This operation is only supported from kernel mode.//  The output of this operation is a USN structure.//// Flags for this operation (none defined)// A list of USN reasons to set//No Flags currently defined// ****************** FSCTL_QUERY_STORAGE_CLASSES ***************************////  The FILE_STORAGE_TIER is an identifier for the storage tier relative to the volume.//  The storage tier ID for a particular volume has no relationship to the storage tier//  ID with the same value on a different volume.//////  Note! The MediaType is used to indicate an uninitialized in-memory DSC structure.//  Do not use values 0xfe or 0xff as media types.//////  These flags *must* match those for _STORAGE_TIER as the file system does a//  pass through.////// Tier ID////// Name for the tier////// Note for the tier////// Flags: FILE_STORAGE_TIER_FLAG_xxx////// Provisioned capacity of the tier////// Media type of the tier////// Classification of the tier//////  This structure has the same fields as STORAGE_DEVICE_TIERING_DESCRIPTOR and//  that structure reserves the upper WORD   of the Flags field for file system use.//  We define the following possible values for the Flags field.//////  The response returns a single structure of FSCTL_QUERY_STORAGE_CLASSES_OUTPUT//  that has all the tiers for this volume.////// Size of this structure serves// as the version////// Size of  this structure  plus// all the variable sized fields////// Flags////// The total number of available tiers for this disk////// The number of tiers that fit in the output////// Detailed info on the storage tiers.//////  Below are flags used by the Reparse union type within STREAM_INFORMATION_ENTRY.//////  This structure lists information on the stream.////// Version of this struct. Current version is 1.////// Flags////// The stream information varies by type of stream. We enclose// the various types in a union.//////  Desired Storage Class//////  Class//////  Flags//////  Total Length of STREAM_INFORMATION_ENTRY structure.//////  Flags (Reserved for future use)//////  Reserved.//////  The Vdl (Valid Data Length) of data stream.//////  Total Length of STREAM_INFORMATION_ENTRY structure.//////  Flags//////  The size of Reparse data buffer.//////  Offset to reparse point data buffer (REPARSE_DATA_BUFFER or REPARSE_GUID_DATA_BUFFER).//////  Total Length of STREAM_INFORMATION_ENTRY structure.//////  Flags (Reserved for future use)//////  The size of Ea.//////  Offset to EA (Extended Attributes) information buffer (FILE_FULL_EA_INFORMATION).//// ****************** FSCTL_QUERY_REGION_INFO *******************************////  Input structure for FSCTL_QUERY_REGION_INFO.////  This FSCTL returns the storage tier regions from the storage//  stack for a particular volume.//// The version of this structure.// The size of this structure in bytes.// Reserved for future use.// Number of entries in TierIds, 0 for all for volume// Storage tiers to return information for////  Structure that describes a single storage tier region.//// Tier ID// offset of region in bytes// length of region in bytes////  Output structure for FSCTL_QUERY_REGION_INFO.////  The FSCTL_QUERY_REGION_INFO response returns a single one of these that include//  multiple FILE_STORAGE_TIER_REGION records, one for each region.//// The version of this structure.// The size of this structure in bytes.// Reserved for future use.// Reserved for future use.// in bytes, must align to slab boundary// Total number of available regions.// Number of regions that fit in the output.// Detailed info on the regions.////  This structure contains the information for the Desired Storage Class attribute.////// Class type of the tier////// Flags//////  This structure has the same fields as STORAGE_DEVICE_TIERING_DESCRIPTOR and//  that structure reserves the upper WORD   of the Flags field for file system use.//  We define the following possible values for the Flags field.//////=============== FSCTL_DUPLICATE_EXTENTS_TO_FILE ====================//////  32/64 Bit thunking support structure//////=============== FSCTL_DUPLICATE_EXTENTS_TO_FILE_EX ==================//////  32/64 Bit thunking support structure//////=============== FSCTL_QUERY_ASYNC_DUPLICATE_EXTENTS_STATUS ==================//////==================== FSCTL_QUERY_REFS_SMR_VOLUME_INFO =======================//////  Fields added in V1//////==================== FSCTL_SET_REFS_SMR_VOLUME_GC_PARAMETERS =======================//////==================== STREAMS CONSTANTS =======================//////==================== FSCTL_STREAMS_QUERY_PARAMETERS =======================//////==================== FSCTL_STREAMS_ASSOCIATE_ID =======================//////==================== FSCTL_STREAMS_QUERY_ID =======================//////=============== FSCTL_QUERY_BAD_RANGES ==================//////  Starting offset of the range in bytes.//////  Length of the range in bytes.//////  Input buffer defining the ranges in the file to look for//  bad ranges.  A NULL input buffer would lookup the entire//  file.//////  Number of ranges given, in the Ranges array.//////  Array of ranges (<Offset,  Length> pairs)  to look for//  bad ranges.  A range with MAXDWORDDWORD as LengthInBytes//  would represent a range till end of the file allocation.//  For that matter a range going beyond allocation size//  will be capped at allocation size.//////  Flags, reserved for future.//////  Starting offset of the range in bytes.//////  Length of the range in bytes.//////  Output buffer defining the bad ranges.//////  Number of bad ranges populated in the Ranges//  array.//////  If the output buffer is not big enough to hold all the//  bad ranges, the FSCTL would get STATUS_BUFFER_OVERFLOW//  with as many bad ranges as  that can fit  in the given//  output buffer and  NextOffsetToLookUp gives the offset//  from which the user can requery the remaining bad ranges.//////  Array of bad ranges (<Offset, Length> pairs) in the//  ranges that user asked for.//////========= FSCTL_SET_DAX_ALLOC_ALIGNMENT_HINT =========////  Once this FSCTL is issued for a file, the following//  allocations to the file will be examined to see if//  the given offset is being allocated and if so the//  allocation will be placed such that the physical//  address corresponding to that allocation is aligned//  to the given AlignmentValue, if we have free space//  available at aligned regions.////  To reset the alignment constraint you can issue this//  FSCTL with AlignmentValue of 1 i.e. AlignmentShift//  as 0.//////  The block size to align the given offset of the file.//  The file is allocated such that the physical address//  corresponding to the file is aligned to this block//  size.  The actual alignment value is given by:////    AlignmentValue = (1 << AlignmentShift);//////  Offset in file that should be aligned to the given//  AlignmentValue.  This should be cluster aligned.////  NOTE: MM supports huge page only if the file offset//  and the physical address are 1gb aligned.//////  If DAX_ALLOC_ALIGNMENT_FLAG_FALLBACK_SPECIFIED is present in//  Flags, this field specifies a fallback block size to align//  the given offset of the file whenever allocation satisfying//  AlignmentShift could not be found.//////  MANDATORY - If allocation satisfying AlignmentShift (or at least//     FallbackAlignmentShift if specified) cannot be found, then fail//     the file system operation (e.g. extending the file).//////  FALLBACK_SPECIFIED - Indicates that the FallbackAlignmentShift field//      is present in the input structure and indicates a fallback//      alignment if the optimal alignment isn't available.//////========= FSCTL_VIRTUAL_STORAGE_SET_BEHAVIOR =========////  Configures file system-specific behaviors for files//  used as backing stored for virtual storage devices.////// EFS DPL key availability data structure for FSCTL_ENCRYPTION_KEY_CONTROL//// Structure header size, usable for structure versioning.// Full structure size.// Byte offset of the key blob relative to the start of this structure.// Could be 0 if key blob is not passed.// Size of the key blob.// Could be 0 if key blob is not passed.// DPL lock/unlock indicator: 1 means lock, 0 means unlock.// DPL user runtime ID for who this control is issued.// DPL credential runtime ID which is being impacted.//// Generic fsctl headers which can be sent to the driver.////// Structures for WIM provider specific fsctl's.//////  WimType specifies a set of flags corresponding to the type of WIM.////  WIM_BOOT_OS_WIM means the wim contains Windows system files.//  WIM_BOOT_NOT_OS_WIM means the wim contains 3rd party files or data files.////  Do not use WIM_BOOT_OS_WIM for data that should be preserved on//  system upgrade.////// Structures for WOF File provider specific fsctl's.//// sizeof(VIRTUALIZATION_INSTANCE_INFO_INPUT_EX)// Total Size of the NotificationInfo Buffer.// Offset from beginning of this struct to the NotificationInfo Buffer.// This should be set to PROJFS_PROTOCOL_VERSION.////  Structures for FSCTL_GET_FILTER_FILE_IDENTIFIER.//// end_ntosifs// begin_ntifs begin_winioctl////============== FSCTL_MANAGE_BYPASS_IO ================////  This FSCTL is used to control BypassIO operations on a given file in the//  filter and file system stacks//////  Defines the various operations supported by the BypassIO FSCTL//////  This request can come from user or kernel mode.  This is a request to enable//  BypassIO for the given file which means a driver may not see all reads/writes//  for that file.////  On the pre-operation, if a driver can support BypassIO for the given file//  it should forward the request down the stack.////  On the pre-operation if a driver CAN NOT support BypassIO for the given//  file they should complete the FSCTL with STATUS_SUCCESS and should call//  FltVetoBypassIo to update the FS_BPIO_OUTPUT structure with appropriate//  OpStatus, FailingDriverName and FailureReason reasons.////  During the post-operation they can see if all drivers below them are//  capable of supporting BypassIO.  If yes, the driver should preserve any//  needed state for the file and continue completion processing. It is the//  filter and file systems responsibility to maintain state to properly//  handle requests that may not be compatible with the BypassIO enabled state.////  During the post-operation processing if a driver determines they can no//  longer support BypassIO, they can  call FltVetoBypassIo to inform the//  stack below them that BypassIO is now disabled.  They should set appropriate//  OpStatus, FailingDriverName and FailureReason reasons as to why it can not//  be supported.//////  This request can come from user or kernel mode.  This informs filters and//  file systems that BypassIO is being disabled on this file.  It allows a//  driver to cleanup any associated BypassIO state.////  If a driver has previously allowed BypassIO to be enabled on this file and//  now needs to turn off BypassIO support for a file (a good example is an encryption//  driver wanting to encrypt this file).  They should send this FSCTL to the top//  of the file system stack using the associated handle.////  This can be received by a driver that currently does not have BypassIO enabled,//  it should be ignored.  If sent on a file that currently does not have BypassIO//  enabled, it should be ignored.////  This operation should not be failed.//////  This request can come from user or kernel mode.  This is an informational//  request to see if BypassIO can be enabled for the given file.  This//  should be processed the same as an ENABLE operation with the appropriate//  fields in the FS_BPIO_OUTPUT structure filled out.  The only difference//  is that the driver does not enter the ENABLE state.//////  This request can come from user or kernel mode.  This is a request to PAUSE//  BypassIO on this volume/storage stack.  All active BypassIO enabled files will//  stop doing storage stack level BypassIO operations.  This can be sent on a//  volume handle or any file handle for the given volume.////  Bitlocker is an example of a component that would use this when it needs to//  enable encryption on a volume.  It is fine to send this even if BypassIO is//  not currently enabled on any files on the volume////  This operation will not return until all active BypassIO files have transitioned//  to normal operating mode and all outstanding Bypass IO's have completed.////  This operation should not be failed.//////  This request can come from user or kernel mode.  This should be sent by a driver//  which has previously PAUSED BypassIO on a volume/storage stack and can//  now allow the stack to continue doing BypassIO.////  Volsnap is an example of a component that would PAUSE BypassIO (via//  FS_BPIO_VOLUME_STACK_PAUSE) when a snapshot is created and would send this when//  the snapshot is deleted.////  This operation should not be failed.//////  This request can come from user or kernel mode.  This is a request to PAUSE//  BypassIO on this stream.  All active BypassIO enabled files will stop doing//  BypassIO operations.  It is fine to send this even if BypassIO is//  not currently enabled on any files on the stream////  This operation will not return until all active BypassIO's on this stream//  have completed and no new BypassIO operations are being generated.////  This operation should not be failed.//////  This request can come from user or kernel mode.  This should be sent by a//  filter which has previously PAUSED BypassIO on this stream and can now//  allow them to be used again.////  This operaiton should not be failed by filters.  The file system will//  determine if Bypass can be resumed on the stream by issuing a FS_BPIO_OP_QUERY//  to the top of the filter stack.//////  This request can come from user or kernel mode.  This returns information//  about the BypassIO state of the volume.//////  All valid operations will be less than this value//////  Defines the BypassIO INPUT flags//////  Only applies to QUERY operations. When set it will suppress sending the//  BypassIO IOCTL to the storage stack.  It will return results based only//  on the filter stack.  This has no effect on ENABLE operations.//////  Defines the INPUT structure for FSCTL_MANAGE_BYPASS_IO//////  The BypassIO operation being requested//////  Input flags for this operation.//////  Reserved fields for future improvements, these fields MUST be set to zero//////  Defines the BypassIO OUTPUT flags//////  When set it means BypassIO has been temporarily PAUSED for this volume//////  When set it means BypassIO has been temporarily PAUSED for this Stream//////  When set, a minifilter has attached to this volume whose supported//  features state says it does not suport BypassIO.  All BypassIO is//  blocked on this volume.//////  When set, the storage driver for this volume is BypassIO compatible.//  This flag is only defined for ENABLE/QUERY/GETINFO operations//////  This structure defines operation specific outputs for both the ENABLE//  and QUERY operations//////  A status code that will be available to users which identifies WHY//  the specified driver can't support BypassIO for this file.  This should//  only be set by the first driver to fail the enable/acquire request.//////  Receives the name of the driver that failed the request.  For diagnostic//  reasons it is required that drivers store their name when failing an//  ENABLE/ASK requests.  The name should match the actual name of the driver//  used by the system with extension.  Ex: "ntfs.sys".////  FailingDriversNameLen contains the length of the string in CHARACTERS.//  No one should assume the string is NULL terminated.//////  Receives a reasonable description of why the driver can not s